e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #Empty-body is only the most obvious “bad JSON” case.
e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #Any of the following will hit the same NULL-pointer path in the current code:
e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #1. Content is completely empty (what you just saw).
e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Content is white-space only (`"   \n  "`).
e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #3. Content is a JSON literal that is not an object (`"foo"`, `42`, `true`, `[]`).
e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #4. Content is a valid object but contains no member named `"id"` (the first thing the RPC
e7b7606 [kimi-k2-0905] #dispatcher touches).
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #5. Content is truncated so the parser never manages to build a JSObject at all (parser returns
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #non-zero but you still dereference the out-pointer).
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #All of these end up with `JSValue::objectMember` or `JSValue::stringMember` being called on a value
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #whose `type == Null` (or on a null `JSObject*`) and the current implementation happily forwards the
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #call to the JSObject instance, hence the SIGSEGV.
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #So the minimal fix in `headless.h` is still:
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #// in JSValue
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #utf8 stringMember(utf8 name) const {
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #    if (type != Object || !object) return "";   // <-- guard
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #    return object->stringMember(name);
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #}
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #and identically for `integerMember`, `numberMember`, etc.
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #That single change makes every bullet above return a graceful “missing” value instead of crashing.
e7b7607 [kimi-k2-0905] #
