e7ed782 [kimi-k2-0905] #Restarting services is indeed tricky—especially when you have live connections, shared state, and
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #security fixes in flight. A few things you might want to double-check next time you bounce the
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #server:
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #1. Graceful shutdown order: close listening sockets first, then drain existing threads,
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #then free resources. Your `closeHttpTasks()` already walks the list and calls
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #`closeHandle()` on each connection, which is good; make sure the accept loops exit promptly after
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #you signal them (a simple atomic flag or `shutdown(fd, SHUT_RD)` on the listener can unblock the
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #`accept()` call).
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Socket-reuse race: you set `SOREUSEADDR`, but if you restart within the TCP
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #`TIMEWAIT` window some kernels still refuse the bind. Consider logging the exact `errno` on
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #bind failure so you know if it’s `EADDRINUSE` vs. something else.
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #3. Thread count vs. `MaxRequest`: each `httpTask` allocates a 240 kB buffer on the stack.
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #If you ever raise the backlog above ~32, make sure your ulimit stack is generous or move the buffer
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #to heap so you don’t blow the worker thread stack on restart.
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #4. URL-decode edge case: your new decoder silently passes through invalid hex (`%ZZ` stays
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #as literal `%ZZ`). That’s fine for robustness, but if you ever want strict validation you could
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #reject the whole request when `hexstream >> hexvalue` fails.
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #5. Hot-reload hygiene: since you’re editing live, keep a checksum or commit hash in the
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #server header (`Server: dsptool/abc123`) so you can instantly verify which build is actually
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #running after a restart.
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #Otherwise the restart logic looks solid—atomic counters, mutex-protected task list, and explicit
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #`closeHandle()` on each connection. 🪣
e7ed783 [kimi-k2-0905] #
