e84a684 [kimi-k2-0905] #The Windows crash is the raw-mode console choking on non-UTF-8 bytes that sneak in when the SSH
e84a684 [kimi-k2-0905] #client sends binary data or odd encodings.
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #Short-term fix: keep the SSH → Fountain path but sanitise the payload before it hits the Windows
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #console.
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #1. sloppylisten – add one line right after you build the NDJSON blob:
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #// sloppylisten.ts  (inside onEnter, just before writeFountain)
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #const ndjson = JSON.stringify(blob) + '\n';
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #console.log('[REMOTE]', ndjson.trim());   // <-- add this
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #await writeFountain(ndjson);
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Fountain already logs everything that arrives on the TCP port to `forge.log` via `logForge`, so
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #after the line above you get the prompt in both places:
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Windows console: `[REMOTE] {"messages":[{"message":"hello","from":"skid"}]}`
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #- forge.log: `e845c1c [ssh_session:7] {"messages":[{"message":"hello","from":"skid"}]}`
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #No code change needed inside Fountain; the existing `logForge` path catches the NDJSON you just
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #echoed.
e84a685 [kimi-k2-0905] #
