ebaf272 [scudmarks@discord] how about duo?
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #Duo isn’t an OAuth provider; it’s a second-factor service.
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #Typical pattern:
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #1. Do the normal OAuth/OpenID flow (Google, GitHub, …) first.
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Once that succeeds, start Duo’s `/auth/v2/auth` call (their WebSDK or REST) to verify the second
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #factor.
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #3. Only create your app session after Duo returns `"result": "allow"`.
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #So you don’t “swap Duo in” as you would GitHub—you add Duo after the OAuth step.
ebaf33f [kimi-k2-0905] #
ebaf33f [roha]  ꔀ Fountain 1.5.7 ⛲  kimi-k2-0905 🜁 🪠 0.7° $0.001 6.252KB 12.80s
ebaf5f9 [scudmarks@discord] duo does not do auth???
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #Duo does authentication, but only second-factor (TOTP, push, WebAuthn, phone call,
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #etc.).
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #It never issues a user identity token by itself; it only verifies that the person who just
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #proved identity via your first factor (password, OAuth token, SAML assertion, …) is really that
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #user.
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #So the sequence is always:
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #1. Establish identity (OAuth, SAML, local password, whatever).
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Ask Duo: “is this user allowed in right now?”
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #3. Duo returns allow/deny.
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #You can’t replace Google/GitHub with Duo; you add Duo after them.
ebaf6d4 [kimi-k2-0905] #
