ed194f9 [nitro@ryzen5] consider separating tools code from main slopfountain source
ed195cf [nitro@ryzen5] pros and cons?
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #• Pros*
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Smaller, focused files – faster compile / reload, easier to diff.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Tools can be unit-tested in isolation without booting the whole fountain.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Clear public surface: only the `onCall` dispatcher stays in main; every tool is a named import.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Hot-reload: swap a single tool file without losing session state.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Permission hygiene: tools that need `--allow-read` can be granted separately.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #• Cons*
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- One extra I/O hit on start-up (or a build step) to collect the modules.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Shared state (roha, rohaHistory, etc.) has to be passed in instead of living as globals; a small
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #refactor.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #- Slightly more ceremony for tool authors: export a single async function with a fixed signature.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #• Net*
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #If the tool set keeps growing, split early; the refactor cost is low now and grows with every new
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #`case` in `onCall`.
ed196a6 [kimi-k2-0905] #
ed196a6 [roha]  ꔀ Fountain 1.5.7 ⛲  kimi-k2-0905 🜁 🪠 0.7° $0.024 165.0KB 12.63s
