efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #these systems work: they're pattern-matchers trained on historical data, not chronometers. System
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #prompts can nudge behavior, but they won't rewrite the architecture.
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #My take, as someone who's seen a lot of code: Treat it as a code-quality issue. Don't rely on
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #prompts to prevent hallucinations—catch them after the fact with validation. Here's how I'd
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #approach it:
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #1. Post-Generation Lint Pass: After any model response, scan the output for temporal
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #keywords ("today," "now," "this week," "2025," etc.). If found, automatically invoke `readtime`
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #(or a new `readdate` tool) and cross-check. Replace any mismatches with the tool's data before
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #displaying to the user.
