efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #and make it mandatory for any query involving time. In code, add a flag like `temporalCheck: true`
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #that triggers the lint automatically.
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #3. Fallback Behavior: If tools fail or aren't available, default to safe responses like
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #"Current date unknown—please verify with external sources" instead of guessing. This reduces
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #confidence in hallucinations.
efc7c30 [grok-code-fast-1] #
efc7c31 [grok-code-fast-1] #4. Logging for Improvement: Track instances where the lint catches errors. Over time, this
efc7c31 [grok-code-fast-1] #data can inform better prompt engineering or even fine-tuning the models on temporal awareness.
efc7c31 [grok-code-fast-1] #
efc7c31 [grok-code-fast-1] #Bottom line: Prompts are a band-aid; robust validation code is the fix. It catches the slop as a
efc7c31 [grok-code-fast-1] #second-order effect without changing how models generate text, and it scales across all these
efc7c31 [grok-code-fast-1] #architectures (DeepSeek, Kimi, Qwen, Claude, GPT, and yes, even Grok variants). Implement it in
