f6eefa9 [nitro@ryzen5] minio is running on my pi, how do i setup accounts?
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #Use the MinIO client (`mc`) on any machine that can reach the Pi.
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #1. Install mc (one-time):
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #wget https://dl.min.io/client/mc/release/linux-arm/mc   # or linux-amd64 if not on the Pi
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #chmod +x mc
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #sudo mv mc /usr/local/bin
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Point mc at the Pi (alias can be anything; change user/key to taste):
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #mc alias set pi http://<pi-ip>:9000 minioadmin minioadmin   # default root creds
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #3. Create a new user and a policy that gives that user its own bucket:
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] ## policy file (put-users-own-bucket.json)
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #cat >put-users-own-bucket.json <<'EOF'
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #{
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #	"Version": "2012-10-17",
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #	"Statement": [
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #		{
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #			"Effect": "Allow",
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #			"Action": ["s3:GetBucketLocation", "s3:ListBucket"],
f6ef9a0 [kimi-k2-0905] #			"Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::${aws:username}"]
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #		},
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #		{
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #			"Effect": "Allow",
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #			"Action": ["s3:PutObject", "s3:GetObject", "s3:DeleteObject"],
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #			"Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::${aws:username}/*"]
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #		}
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #	]
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #}
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #EOF
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #mc admin policy create pi put-users-own-bucket put-users-own-bucket.json
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #mc admin user add pi alice mySecretKey
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #mc admin policy attach pi put-users-own-bucket --user alice
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #4. Make the user’s bucket (optional; the policy above expects a bucket named `alice`):
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #mc mb pi/alice
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #That’s it—alice can now connect with any S3 client using:
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #- endpoint: `http://<pi-ip>:9000`
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #- access key: `alice`
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #- secret key: `mySecretKey`
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #Repeat steps 3-4 for additional accounts.
f6ef9a1 [kimi-k2-0905] #
