f939b0e [nitro@ryzen5] what are options for hooking v8 to native sockets , i suppose follow node convention?
f939cdb [kimi-k2-0905] #Three practical ways, in descending order of effort:
f939cdb [kimi-k2-0905] #
f939cdb [kimi-k2-0905] #1. Re-use Node’s implementation
f939cdb [kimi-k2-0905] #   Clone the Node repo, lift `src/tcpwrap.cc`, `src/connectionwrap.cc`,
f939cdb [kimi-k2-0905] #`src/streambase-inl.h`, and the `uvpoll_t` integration.
f939cdb [kimi-k2-0905] #   Pro: battle-tested, keeps the same JS API (`net.Socket`, `net.Server`).
f939cdb [kimi-k2-0905] #   Con: drags in libuv and a lot of Node scaffolding.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Roll your own minimal binding with libuv (same reactor loop Node uses)
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   - Add `uvtcpt` handles to your isolate’s embedder slot.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   - Expose `tcp.connect(port, host, cb)`, `tcp.onread`, `tcp.write(buf)` in a global `native_net`
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #object.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   - Call `uvrun(loop, UVRUN_NOWAIT)` once per turn of the micro-task queue.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   Keeps the event-driven style without pulling all of Node.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #3. Zero-deps, blocking-only wrapper (good for quick demos)
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   - Write a simple `int socket_connect(const char* host, int port)` etc. in C.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   - Wrap with `FunctionTemplate`, return an integer fd to JS.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   - Expose `read(fd, buf, len)` / `write(fd, buf, len)` that do plain `::recv`/`::send`.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #   No reactor, no threading—JS thread blocks; fine for CLI tools, useless for servers.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #Pick the layer that matches how much of Node’s behaviour you want to re-implement; there is no
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #“official” V8 socket API—every embedder wires up its own I/O integration.
f939cdc [kimi-k2-0905] #
