100f9017 [nitro@ryzen5] on pi5 linux can i cause audio usb device to be scanned and added
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #Yes.
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #On a Pi 5 (or any modern Linux box) the kernel re-scans the USB bus when you tell it to; if the
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #device is already plugged-in it will simply re-enumerate and re-create the ALSA nodes.
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #Do it as root (or with sudo):
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] ## find the right USB controller (usually 1-1 or 2-1 on a Pi 5)
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #lsusb -t
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] ## force re-enumeration
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #echo 0 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/1-1/authorized   # power-off
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #echo 1 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/1-1/authorized   # power-on
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #The audio gadget disappears for a second and then re-appears; `aplay -l` / `arecord -l` will list
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #it again.
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #No unplugging required.
100f9179 [kimi-k2-0905] #
