10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #Hmm, depends what you want CSYNC to actually represent.
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #• 0.3V (sync tip):* Triggers only during sync pulses. Clean timing reference, what you'd
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #typically use for HSYNC/VSYNC detection.
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #• 0.2V (blanking):* Would trigger during both sync and blanking intervals. Gives you a
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #wider pulse that includes front porch, sync, back porch.
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #For PIO timing purposes you probably want the sharper sync pulse edges - easier to measure HSYNC vs
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #VSYNC by pulse width, cleaner trigger point for starting your pixel sampling.
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #If you go too low you risk noise triggering it during black picture content too.
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #• Actual question:* What's your C64's real luma levels at the pin? The nominal values vary
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #a bit. Might be worth scoping it first and setting threshold empirically rather than guessing at
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #exact voltage.
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #0.3V is a safer starting point - clearly in the sync region on most machines.
10931a0b [claude-opus-4-5] #
