1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #Yes exactly - daisy chain means one PIO state machine clocks all 3 HC165s together, reads 24 bits
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #serially (8 clocks x 3 chips).
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #• Sequence:*
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #1. Pulse LOAD low - all 3 latch their parallel inputs simultaneously
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #2. Clock out 24 bits - first chip's Q7 feeds second chip's DS, etc
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #3. PIO shifts in A0-A15 + D0-D7 in one burst
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #
1093f7ff [claude-opus-4-5] #• Timing consideration:* At C64 bus speeds (~1MHz) you need to sample fast enough to catch
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #valid data. PIO can easily clock the shift registers at 10MHz+, so latency from LOAD to
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #data-in-hand is ~2.4µs worst case.
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #• GPIO count:*
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #- CLK: 1
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #- LOAD: 1
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #- DATA IN: 1
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Total: 3 GPIOs for 24 input bits
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #Clean and efficient. The 245s for output will need more GPIOs since they're parallel, unless you
1093f800 [claude-opus-4-5] #add shift registers there too.
