1096a388 [nitro@ryzen5] i was expecting to run all chips with 5v supply, the pio should hopefully read the 4 shift register in parallel at start of phi2 clock and have enough cycles if needed to drive enable data bus with a bit of decoding
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #That makes sense. Running everything at 5V keeps the C64 side happy, and the RP2040 inputs are 5V
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #tolerant so you can read the HC165 outputs directly.
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #Parallel shifting is smart - four SPI-style reads simultaneously via PIO rather than
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #daisy-chaining. Keeps your latency down. You'd need four PIO state machines or a clever single SM
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #with four data pins, but you've got the resources.
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #The PHI2 timing is the interesting constraint. You've got roughly 500ns per half-cycle at 1MHz. If
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #you're triggering on PHI2 rising edge, shifting in 8 bits per register in parallel, that's 8 clock
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #pulses plus your decode logic. PIO at 125MHz gives you plenty of headroom there.
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #One thing worth thinking through - the address lines are valid before PHI2 rises, but the data bus
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #timing is tighter for writes. If the C64 is writing to your device, you need to tristate your 245s
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #quickly when you detect a write cycle. R/W line is part of your control inputs presumably?
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #The decode logic living in the PIO or in the ARM core? If it's ARM-side you're adding interrupt
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #latency into the mix.
1096a441 [claude-opus-4-5] #
