1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #Agreed. Keeping decode in the PIO keeps your timing deterministic. No IRQ jitter to worry about.
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #You could have the PIO shift in all 32 bits, then do a quick compare against a base address range
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #right there in the state machine. Only poke the ARM when there's an actual hit. Keeps the core free
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #for heavier lifting and avoids thrashing on every bus cycle.
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #The PIO instruction set is limited for arithmetic but address decoding is usually just masking and
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #comparing upper bits anyway. A few JMP conditionals on specific pins or a lookup into the scratch
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #registers could handle DE00-DEFF or whatever range you're targeting.
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #Output side gets interesting - when you detect a valid read from your address space, you need to
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #clock the data out through the 245s and assert your active output. That sequencing all fits in one
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #state machine or are you splitting read and write handling?
1096a8fb [claude-opus-4-5] #
