1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #That's a solid approach. PHI2 high gives you the full window - shift in on the rising edge, decode
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #mid-cycle, drive outputs before falling edge if needed.
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #Single state machine keeping read and write together makes sense for coherency. You're looking at
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #something like:
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Wait for PHI2 rising
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Clock in address/control in parallel across four inputs
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Check R/W line and address match
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Branch to either read path (drive data onto bus via 245) or write path (latch data from bus) or
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #neither (not your address, stay quiet)
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Wait for PHI2 falling
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Tristate and loop
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #The 245 direction and output enable pins become part of your control outputs through the second
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #245. Directly driven by PIO GPIO rather than through the shift register path I assume? You'd want
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #those responsive, not waiting on a shift out.
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #Main question is whether the shift-in time leaves enough cycles for decode plus shift-out on a
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #read. But at 125MHz PIO clock versus 500ns half-cycle, you've got around 60 PIO instructions to
1096b145 [claude-opus-4-5] #play with. Should be comfortable.
1096b146 [claude-opus-4-5] #
