10976a71 [nitro@ryzen5] only data bus needs the /OE what do i do with control pull downs? is that what they are?
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #The control signals (/IRQ, /NMI, /RESET, /ROML, /ROMH, /EXROM) are active-low, directly mapped to
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #C64 signals. They need to be driven appropriately by the Pico.
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #The C64 typically expects these as active-low, active-high, active-low, active-low, active-low,
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #active-low respectively.
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #Typically cartridges use open-collector/open-drain outputs on these lines so multiple devices can
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #share them without bus contention. The C64 has internal pull-ups on most of these.
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #So rather than push-pull from the 245, you might want:
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #- Default state: high-Z (let C64 pull-ups hold them high)
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #- Active state: pull low when asserting
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #The 245 with /OE gives you that - tristate when inactive, drive low when active. So you do want /OE
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #control on transceiver 6 as well.
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #Alternatively, external pull-downs aren't what you want - you want the C64's pull-ups to do the
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #work, and only drive low when needed.
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #Keep /OE on both transceivers:
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #GP22, /OE data
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #GP28, /OE ctrl
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #That way control lines float until you need to assert them.
10976b0d [claude-opus-4-5] #
