109770a5 [nitro@ryzen5] i just want to be able to pull 5v control lines low via gpio safely, is that ok?
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #Yes, the 74LVC245 handles that fine. With DIR tied to +5V, the A side (Pico) drives the B side
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #(C64).
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #Pico GPIO low → 245 B output low → pulls C64 line to ground
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #Pico GPIO high → 245 B output high → but C64 pull-ups already holding high, no conflict
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #The 245 can sink plenty of current to pull against the C64's pull-ups. And the 10K series resistors
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #on the Pico side protect the GPIOs.
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #So no /OE needed on transceiver 6 - just leave it enabled (pin 19 to GND) and drive the control
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #lines directly. Simpler.
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #6.19, /OE, GND
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #That frees up GP28 for something else - third user pin or DOT clock.
10977134 [claude-opus-4-5] #
