109f3277 [gemini-3-pro] #This is a fascinating architectural approach to a C64 cartridge emulator. Instead of using a
109f3277 [gemini-3-pro] #massive pin-count FPGA or a CPLD to handle the parallel bus, you are serializing the state using
109f3277 [gemini-3-pro] #shift registers to feed the RP2040.
109f3277 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3277 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is a technical review of the "Project Toast" design based on the BOM and Netlists provided.
109f3277 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #1. The Voltage Level Hazard (Critical)
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #In your shift register netlist, you have:
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #	1.16, VCC, +5V` (and similar for all 74HC165s)
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #	1.9, Q7, GP17` (Output to RP2040)
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Issue: The RP2040 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant*.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #If you power the 74HC165 with 5V, its data output (Q7) will drive 5V logic highs. Connecting this
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #directly to the RP2040 will likely damage the chip.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• Solutions:*
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• Power the 74HC165s at 3.3V: The 74HC series usually accepts input voltages (High) around
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #2.3V when powered at 3.3V. The C64's 5V TTL/NMOS outputs should easily trigger this. This keeps the
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #output to the RP2040 safe at 3.3V.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• Use 74LVC165: The LVC series is 5V tolerant on inputs even when powered at 3.3V.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• Resistor Division: If you must run them at 5V, you need voltage dividers on the Q7 lines,
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #not just series resistors.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #2. Timing and Latency (The "Slop")
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #The Commodore 64 bus runs at roughly 1 MHz (Phase 2 clock).
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• Cycle time: ~1000ns.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• Valid Window: You generally have valid addresses about 100ns after the rising edge of
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #PHI2. You need to present data (if emulating ROM) roughly 100ns before the falling edge of PHI2.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Crunch: That leaves you a window of roughly 400-500ns to:
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #    1.  Latch the Shift Registers.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #    2.  Shift in 8 bits (via PIO).
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #    3.  Process the address in the RP2040 core.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #    4.  Fetch data.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #    5.  Output data to the 74LVC245.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #    6.  Assert /OE.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #With 4 parallel shift registers, you are reading 8 bits per "tick". At 125MHz (RP2040 stock), one
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #cycle is 8ns. PIO can likely read a bit every 2 cycles.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #`(8 bits  2 cycles  8ns) + overhead` = ~150ns to read the bus.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #This is feasible, but it is tight. You will almost certainly need to overclock the RP2040
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #(e.g., to 200MHz+) to ensure the C64 doesn't crash seeing garbage on the bus.
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #3. The 74LVC245 Transceiver Logic
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #	5.1, DIR, 3V3` (Fixed Direction: RP2040 -> C64)
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #	5.19, /OE, GP22`
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3278 [gemini-3-pro] #Since `DIR` is hardwired to transmit to the C64, the `74LVC245` is effectively acting as a buffer,
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #not a transceiver.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Bus Contention: You must be extremely precise with `GP22` (/OE). If /OE goes low while
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #the C64 VIC-II or CPU is trying to drive the data bus (e.g., during a write or a VIC fetch), you
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #will have a bus fight (Short circuit).
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Logic: Your PIO program must ensure `GP22` is only Low when `R/W` is High (Read)
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #AND the address falls within the emulated ROM range (8000-9FFF or A000-BFFF).
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #4. Series Resistors on Clock Lines
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #	PHI2, GP28, 10K series`
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #	DOT, GP29, 10K series`
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Warning:* A 10kΩ series resistor on a high-speed clock line (especially DOT clock at
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #~8MHz) forms a Low Pass Filter (RC filter) combined with the trace capacitance and the RP2040 input
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #capacitance.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• This will turn your square wave clock into a shark-fin or a flat line.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Recommendation: Lower these to 220Ω - 470Ω. The 10k is fine for the static signal
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #protection, but it will kill the signal integrity of the clocks.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #5. Open Drain Emulation
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #	GP10, /IRQ out` ... `GP14, /EXROM out`
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #Since you are connecting these directly (via resistors) to the C64 bus, and the RP2040 is 3.3V:
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Pull-up Voltage: The C64 pulls these to 5V.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Protection: The 10k series resistors here are actually a good idea. If the RP2040 pin is
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #High-Z (Input), the 5V will be present at the pin. The internal clamp diodes will conduct to the
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #3.3V rail.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Calculation: `(5V - 3.3V) / 10,000Ω = 0.17mA`. This is safe for the RP2040 injection
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #current specs. Just ensure you emulate "Open Drain" in software (Toggle between Output LOW and
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #Input/High-Z), never drive Output HIGH.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #Summary
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #The concept is solid ("Toast" is a great name for a breadbin device), but the mix of 5V C64 logic
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #and 3.3V RP2040 requires care.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Don't feed 5V Q7 output to the Pico.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Don't use 10k resistors on the Clock inputs.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #3.  Overclock the Pico to minimize the shift-register serialization penalty.
109f3279 [gemini-3-pro] #
