109f4ae8 [nitro@ryzen5] # GPIO connectionsFor 5V Open Drain GPIO should Toggle between Output LOW and Input/High-ZFor 5V static input protext GPIO with 10K in seriesFor 5V clocks
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is a review of the "Project Toast" netlist and BOM, specifically looking for "slop" (potential
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #electrical issues or edge cases) regarding the 5V/3.3V interface and timing.
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #1. Shift Registers (74HC165) Power & Logic Levels
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #• The 5V Risk: You have the 74HC165s powered by +5V (Pin 16). This means the Q7
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #output (Pin 9) sent to the RP2040 (GP17-20) will be a 5V logic high.
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Issue: The RP2040 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant. Sending 5V directly into them
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #will likely damage the input buffers, even with series resistors (though 10k helps limit current,
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #it clamps the voltage via internal diodes).
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Fix: Use a voltage divider on the four Q7 lines before they reach the RP2040, or
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #power the shift registers with 3.3V (but ensure the specific 74HC chips used are 5V tolerant on
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #inputs, or switch to 74LVC165).
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #2. Clock Signals (DOT & PHI2)
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #• RC Filtering: You noted "For 5V clocks" but listed 10k series resistors for PHI2 (GP28)
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #and DOT (GP29).
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   DOT Clock (Start of Slop): The C64 DOT clock runs at ~8MHz. A 10kΩ resistor
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #combined with the trace/pin capacitance (approx 5-10pF) creates a Low Pass Filter with a cutoff
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #frequency around 1.5MHz - 3MHz. This will completely wash out the 8MHz DOT signal.
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   PHI2: At ~1MHz, a 10k resistor might pass the signal, but the edges will be very
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #rounded, leading to potential timing jitter.
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Recommendation: For clocks, use a resistive voltage divider (e.g., 2.2kΩ series /
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #3.3kΩ to ground) rather than a single series resistor. This creates a clean 3V signal and maintains
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #sharper edges.
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #3. Open Drain / Output Control (IRQ, NMI, ROM lines)
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #• Logic approach: Toggling between `Output LOW` and `Input (High-Z)` is the correct
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #software approach for Open Drain simulation.
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #• Hardware protection: Since these lines are pulled up to 5V inside the C64:
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   When the RP2040 is in `Input` mode, the pin sees 5V.
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   The 10kΩ series resistor is essential here to limit current through the RP2040's
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #internal ESD protection diodes. While this works in "slop" designs, be aware it relies on the
109f4cf7 [gemini-3-pro] #RP2040 clamping the voltage to 3.3V+0.3V.
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #4. Transceiver (74LVC245)
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Direction: Pin 1 (DIR) is tied to 3.3V. This locks the transceiver to Write Only
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #(RP2040 -> C64).
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Bus Contention: Ensure the RP2040 code manages `/OE` (GP22) strictly. If the C64 CPU
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #tries to write to memory while the RP2040 is driving the bus (because `/OE` is low), you will have
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #a bus fight. Since the C64 is NMOS/TTL and the LVC is CMOS, the LVC will likely win, but it
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #stresses the components.
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #5. GPIO Allocation Update
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #• DOT Clock: The netlist text mentions `DOT, GP10` in the "Direct inputs" section, but the
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #allocation list says `GP10, /IRQ`. Later, the updated section lists `DOT, GP29`.
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Correction: Ensure DOT is physically routed to GP29 (or a free pin) as GP10
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #is occupied by /IRQ.
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #Summary of "Slop" to watch:
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Q7 Lines: 5V output into RP2040 (Needs division).
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  DOT Clock: 10k resistor is too high for 8MHz (Needs divider/lower resistance).
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #3.  Clamping: Relying on internal diodes for the static inputs works for hobby use but
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #generates heat/stress inside the MCU silicon.
109f4cf8 [gemini-3-pro] #
