109fd764 [gemini-3-pro] #This looks like a solid architecture for a "Bus Sniffing" cartridge, particularly the move to
109fd764 [gemini-3-pro] #parallelize the shift registers to reduce read latency.
109fd764 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fd764 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is the "slop" detected on port 8081 regarding the BOM and GPIO constraints:
109fd764 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fd764 [gemini-3-pro] #1. BOM vs. Netlist Mismatch
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Slop: The BOM header lists "3× 74HC165" but the logic requires 4
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #(Address Low, Address High, Data, Control).
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Fix: The Netlist correctly defines 4 units. Ensure the order quantity matches the
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #Netlist ($8.40 calculation implies 4 units, so just a typo in the count).
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #2. GP29 Availability (Critical)
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Slop: The Netlist assigns DOT clock to GP29.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Context: The BOM specifies "Pico socket". On a standard Raspberry Pi Pico, GP29
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #is not broken out to the headers (it is used internally for VSYS measurement/mode).
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Fix: You must move DOT clock to a different pin or solder directly to the RP2040 chip
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #if using a custom board.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #       Suggestion:* If you don't need `UART` for debugging, move DOT to GP0 or GP1.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #Alternatively, reclaim `ADC0` (GP26) if analog isn't strictly necessary.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #3. Shift Register Input Protection
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Slop: The Netlist powers the 74HC165s at 3.3V. The C64 bus is 5V.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Physics: This relies on the internal clamping diodes of the 74HC165.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Fix: The "10k series resistors" mentioned in the BOM are mandatory between
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #the C64 bus and the 74HC165 inputs. Without them, the 5V bus will fry the 3.3V-powered shift
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #registers or lift the 3.3V rail.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #4. Open Drain Logic Levels
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Slop: Pulling C64 lines (IRQ/NMI) low via RP2040 GPIO.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Detail: When the RP2040 releases the line (Input/High-Z), the C64 pull-up pulls it to
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #5V. The RP2040 is not 5V tolerant on inputs; its protection diodes will clamp the line to ~3.8V
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #(3.3V + 0.5V).
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Fix: While 3.8V is a valid Logic High for the C64 (TTL), this constantly drains
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #current through the RP2040 diodes. Ensure the 10k resistors are also applied to these OD
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #output lines to limit that clamping current.
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #Summary
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #The logic is sound, but GP29 is the showstopper if you are using a standard Pico. Move DOT
109fd765 [gemini-3-pro] #to GP26 (currently allocated to ADC0) to resolve this.
109fd766 [gemini-3-pro] #
