109fed3e [nitro@ryzen5] option is 2N7000 N-Channel FET ($0.90) for the 7 open drain outputs?
109fef83 [gemini-3-pro] #Yes, using 2N7000 N-Channel FETs is a robust solution for the 7 open-drain outputs.
109fef83 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fef83 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is an assessment of that option versus the current netlist approach:
109fef83 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fef83 [gemini-3-pro] #• 2N7000 Implementation*
109fef83 [gemini-3-pro] #• Voltage Safety: It fully isolates the RP2040 GPIO (3.3V) from the C64 bus voltage (5V).
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• Logic Level: It ensures a strong logic `LOW` (0V) on the C64 bus.
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• Logic Inversion: Note that this inverts your control logic.
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   GPIO HIGH (3.3V): FET Conducts → C64 Line pulled LOW.
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   GPIO LOW (0V): FET Off → C64 Line Floats (pulled up by C64).
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• Why this is better than Series Resistors*
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #Your current BOM suggests 10kΩ series resistors for protection. While this protects the RP2040
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #inputs, it creates a voltage divider when driving outputs.
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• The C64 internal pull-ups (often ~3kΩ to 10kΩ) combined with your 10kΩ series resistor will
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #likely prevent the RP2040 from pulling the voltage below the TTL Logic Low threshold (0.8V).
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• The 2N7000 eliminates this divider issue.
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• Alternative Suggestion*
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #If board space is a concern, consider using a 74LVC07A (Hex Buffer with Open-Drain Outputs)
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #or 74LVC06A (Hex Inverter with Open-Drain Outputs).
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• Pros: Replaces 6 discrete FETs with one SOIC-14 chip; 5V tolerant inputs/outputs.
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #• Cons: You need 7 lines, so you would need two chips (or 1 chip + 1 discrete FET).
109fef84 [gemini-3-pro] #
