10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #Yes, using 2N7000 N-Channel FETs is the superior option for the Open Drain control signals
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #in this context.
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #Here is the technical reasoning and a verification of the updated netlist.
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #Why the 2N7000 is better than Direct GPIO
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #1.  5V Tolerance: The C64 bus is 5V. The RP2040 is strictly 3.3V. While internal pull-ups
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #on the C64 are weak, connecting a 3.3V pin (even in Open Drain/Input mode) to a 5V rail relies on
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #the RP2040's internal protection diodes, which is risky ("slop"). The 2N7000 isolates the RP2040
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #completely from the 5V rail.
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Safety: If your software crashes and leaves a pin in the wrong state, the FET protects
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #the MCU from high voltage.
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #3.  Drive Strength: The 2N7000 Vgs(th) (Gate Threshold Voltage) is typically 2.1V (max 3V).
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #The RP2040's 3.3V output is sufficient to switch the FET fully "ON" for the low currents required
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #to pull down C64 signal lines (typically <5mA).
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #Netlist Review Notes
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Logic Inversion:
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #    With the 2N7000, your logic is now inverted compared to a direct connection.
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #    *   GPIO HIGH (3.3V) → FET ON → Drain connects to GND → C64 Signal LOW
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #(Active).
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #    *   GPIO LOW (0V) → FET OFF → Drain Floating → C64 Signal HIGH
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #(Inactive/Pull-up).
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #       Make sure your firmware initialization sets these GPIOs LOW immediately on boot to
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #prevent accidental bus asserts.*
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #
10a023cf [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Shift Registers (74HC165):
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   You are running VCC at 3.3V.
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Input signals are 5V via 10kΩ resistors.
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   The 10kΩ resistors are crucial here. Without them, 5V into a 74HC chip powered at 3.3V
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #would trigger internal clamping diodes. The resistors limit the current to safe levels.
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Optimization: If you haven't bought chips yet, 74LVC165 or 74AHC165
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #are 5V tolerant on inputs even when VCC is 3.3V, allowing you to potentially drop the input
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #resistors (though keeping them for protection is never a bad idea).
10a023d0 [gemini-3-pro] #
