10a0ad62 [nitro@ryzen5] how fast can pio shift the register?
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #1. The Commodore 1540
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Yes, the 1540 existed.*
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Target Machine: It was designed specifically for the VIC-20.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Speed: It runs slightly faster than the well-known 1541.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• C64 Compatibility: It is generally incompatible with a stock C64. The C64's
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #VIC-II video chip steals cycles from the CPU to draw the screen ("bad lines"), causing the C64 to
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #miss bits from the 1540 because the drive sends data too fast.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Fix: The 1541 was released with modified ROMs to slow the data transfer down to
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #accommodate the C64's video interrupts. You can use a 1540 on a C64 only if you disable the C64's
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #video output (blank the screen) via software.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #2. RP2040 PIO Speed capabilities
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #The RP2040 Programmable I/O (PIO) is significantly faster than the 74HC165 logic or the IEC bus
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #requires.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Theoretical PIO Speed:*
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Clock: Runs at system clock (default 125MHz).
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Throughput: Can shift 1 bit per clock cycle (125Mbps).
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Loop: A typical "bit-bang" loop to read a shift register (Set Clock High, Read Data, Set
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #Clock Low) takes ~2-4 cycles.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Result: The PIO can easily attempt to drive the shift clock at 30MHz to 60MHz.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Hardware Bottleneck (74HC165):*
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #The limiting factor is the 74HC165 chip itself, not the RP2040.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• At 2.0V: Max freq is ~6MHz.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• At 4.5V: Max freq is ~30MHz.
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• IEC Bus Speed: The standard C64 serial bus runs at roughly 1–2 kB/s (standard) or
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #up to 10–20 kB/s (fast loaders).
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe8 [gemini-3-pro] #• Conclusion:* The PIO will spend 99.9% of its time waiting. It is overkill in the best
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #possible way.
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #Project Fifteen Feedback
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #• 5V Logic Warning:*
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #The IEC bus operates at 5V. The RP2040 GPIO is 3.3V and is not 5V tolerant.
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Reading (74HC165):
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   If you power the 74HC165 with 5V: The `Q7` output sent to the RP2040 will be 5V.
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #This will damage the RP2040. You need a voltage divider or level shifter on the data line.
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   If you power the 74HC165 with 3.3V: The 5V signals from the IEC bus entering the
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #shift register inputs might exceed the datasheet absolute maximum (VCC + 0.5V). However, standard
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #74HC inputs usually have clamping diodes. With your 10kΩ series resistors (from Project Toast
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #logic), this is likely safe, as the current will be limited.
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Writing (2N7000):
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   This is the correct design. The Gate is driven by 3.3V (RP2040), the Source is GND, and the
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #Drain connects to the 5V IEC line. The FET handles the voltage difference perfectly.
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #• GPIO Usage Note:*
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #The IEC bus has 5 signal lines: `ATN`, `CLK`, `DATA`, `RESET`, `SRQ` (SRQ is unconnected on C64,
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #used on C128).
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #Since the RP2040 has ~26 GPIOs and you aren't doing the 44-pin cartridge logic in this specific
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #project, you could technically wire the 5 IEC lines directly to GPIOs (with voltage dividers for
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #protection) and skip the Shift Register entirely to reduce BOM count and latency, unless you are
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #daisy-chaining this with Project Toast.
10a0afe9 [gemini-3-pro] #
