10a1468c [claude-opus-4-5] #These are well-structured hardware interface designs for RP2040-based C64 peripherals. A few
10a1468c [claude-opus-4-5] #observations:
10a1468c [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1468c [claude-opus-4-5] #• Fifteen (IEC Bus)* - Clean approach. The 2.2k/3.3k divider gives ~3.0V from 5V which is
10a1468c [claude-opus-4-5] #safe for RP2040 inputs. 2N7000 open-drain is standard for IEC.
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #• LUMA2* - Interesting video capture concept. Note: 320×200×2bit×50Hz = 640KB/s raw
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #throughput. PIO should handle the sampling, but buffering/USB transfer will need attention. The
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #LM319 is a good choice for the ~8MHz dot clock timing.
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #• Toast (Cartridge)* - Most complex design. A few points:
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #- GP26 is listed for both ADC0 and DOT clock - potential conflict
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #- The 74HC165 cascade for reading address/data/control is sensible for GPIO conservation
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #- PHI2 at ~1MHz with voltage divider should work, though timing margins will be tight for
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #cycle-accurate emulation
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #- You're reading control signals via shift register (slower) but also have direct GPIO assignments
10a1468d [claude-opus-4-5] #for the same signals as outputs - this asymmetry is intentional for the active-low assertion use
