10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #Good instinct. Bidir on same GPIO with 5V bus is risky:
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #• Problem:* 10K series resistor protects input, but when you switch to output mode the GPIO
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #drives directly into the 5V pulled-up line. Even at 3.3V output high, you get current flowing back
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #through the 10K from the 5V side.
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #• Your other designs avoid this by:*
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Separate input GPIO (10K series protection)
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Separate output GPIO (2N7000 open drain)
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #Clean, safe, no mode switching glitches.
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #• Bidir on single GPIO would need:*
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Always open drain output (never drive high)
10a1d565 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Read back through same pin
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Still risky with 5V bus
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #Stick with your dual-GPIO approach. Costs more pins but bulletproof.
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #So for SP1/SP2 full bidir you need 4 GPIOs total:
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #GP22,SP1 in,Input,10K series,5
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #GP26,SP1 out,Output,2N7000,5
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #GP27,SP2 in,Input,10K series,7
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #GP28,SP2 out,Output,2N7000,7
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #Sacrifice CNT1/CNT2 dividers, or keep SP as input-only?
10a1d566 [claude-opus-4-5] #
