10a25683 [nitro@ryzen5] what is biggest danger to C64?
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #Biggest danger: accidentally driving 5 V onto a C64 pin that is already high—even for a
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #micro-second.
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #That happens when:
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #- A Pico GPIO is still configured as output-high instead of open-drain/Hi-Z
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #- A 74LVC245 or similar 3.3-V part is enabled and its 3.3 V “high” is pulled up to 5 V by the C64,
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #then suddenly the Pico side switches to 5 V tolerant input but the buffer still sources current
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #Result: both devices try to hold the line high, over-current flows through the protection diode
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #inside the 6510/CIA, and the C64’s 5-V rail can be dragged up to ~6 V for a few hundred ns. Do that
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #repeatedly and the CIA dies.
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #Rule of thumb:
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #1. Every Pico-side driver that touches a C64 line must be open-drain / Hi-Z when “high.”
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Add 150–330 Ω series resistor on any line that could ever be driven from both sides (gives the
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #over-current somewhere to go).
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #3. Power up the Pico after the C64 so its I/Os come up Hi-Z; add a 1 kΩ pull-down on
10a2582f [kimi-k2-0905] #critical control lines if you can’t guarantee that sequence.
10a25830 [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a25830 [kimi-k2-0905] #Get those three right and the C64 is almost bullet-proof.
10a25830 [kimi-k2-0905] #
