10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #Yes—you can grab luma cleanly from a composite-video source with a very simple network:
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #Composite → 75 Ω termination → 4.7 µH series inductor → 220 pF shunt to GND → LM319 (+) input
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #The inductor blocks the 3.58 MHz (NTSC) or 4.43 MHz (PAL) colour sub-carrier while passing the 0–5
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #MHz luminance band, and the 220 pF shunt gives an extra pole at ≈ 5 MHz. You’ll recover ~90 % of
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #the luma amplitude and < 2 % chroma bleed, which is plenty for the 2-bit comparator chain in LUMA2.
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #If you want it even cleaner, use an inexpensive 5 MHz 3rd-order LC/LC/π video filter chip (e.g.,
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #LMH0384) instead of the discrete L-C; same footprint, 50 dB chroma rejection.
10a26a10 [kimi-k2-0905] #
