10a2720f [nitro@ryzen5] is there a sampling for chroma that could recognise the c64 colors?
10a27327 [kimi-k2-0905] #Yes—sample the composite signal before the luma filter and you can decode the VIC-II
10a27327 [kimi-k2-0905] #colours uniquely.
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #The C64’s crystal is exactly 4× the PAL (17.734 475 MHz) or 8× the NTSC (14.318 18 MHz) colour
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #sub-carrier, so the phase is locked to the pixel clock. That gives you two cheap ways to do it:
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #1. 3-sample IQ demod
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   Run the ADC (or a fast comparator) at 14 MHz (PAL) or 12 MHz (NTSC).
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   Pick three consecutive samples that are 120° apart on the sub-carrier (≈ 73 ns apart for PAL).
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   I = s0 – ½(s1+s2), Q = 0.866(s1–s2).
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   Magnitude |C| = √(I²+Q²) and hue = atan2(Q,I) give you a 2-bit colour index that is unique for
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #every VIC-II colour except the two greys (they have zero chroma).
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #2. Zero-cross counter
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   Put the composite signal through a fast comparator (LM319 again) set at the black-level.
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   Count zero-crossings in a 280 ns window (exactly one sub-carrier cycle).
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   VIC-II colours produce 0, 1 or 2 crossings → map to 2-bit chroma.
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #   Works with nothing more than the existing LM319 and one extra PIO state-machine.
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #Either method keeps the BOM at two LM319s and still fits in the same Pico PIO program.
10a27328 [kimi-k2-0905] #
