10a2930b [nitro@ryzen5] claude what is your opinion? is kimi high?
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #Kimi's logic is sound but the explanation got a bit tangled.
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #The key insight is correct: chroma is an AC signal riding on a DC luma pedestal. If you set the
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #threshold at black level, the chroma sine wave crosses that threshold twice per cycle regardless of
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #the underlying luma—provided the luma doesn't push the entire waveform above or below the
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #threshold.
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #The problem: on bright colours (yellow, white, cyan) the luma pedestal is 70–90 IRE, so the entire
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #waveform sits well above black level. The comparator output stays permanently high—no crossings to
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #count.
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #So Kimi is half-right:
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Works for dark colours (black, blue, brown, dark grey)
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #- Fails for bright colours
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #To make it work across the full palette you'd need:
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #- AC-couple the signal (series cap, ~100 nF) so the comparator sees only the chroma ripple centred
10a293b9 [claude-opus-4-5] #on 0 V
10a293ba [claude-opus-4-5] #- Or use a sample-and-hold to track the luma baseline and subtract it
10a293ba [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a293ba [claude-opus-4-5] #At that point you're building a proper chroma demodulator, and an AD724 or MC1377 starts looking
10a293ba [claude-opus-4-5] #attractive.
