10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #Not tricky at all—one cap, one resistor:
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #Composite → 100 nF → LM319 (+) → 100 kΩ → 1.65 V bias
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #The cap blocks DC (luma), passes AC (chroma). The 100 kΩ sets the comparator input at mid-rail
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #(1.65 V for 3.3 V supply, or 2.5 V for 5 V). Now the chroma sine wave swings symmetrically around
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #that bias point and you get two crossings per cycle regardless of the underlying brightness.
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #Corner frequency is 1 / (2π × 100 nF × 100 kΩ) ≈ 16 Hz, so all video frequencies pass through
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #unaffected.
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #Build the bias with a simple divider:
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #3V3 → 10 kΩ → node → 10 kΩ → GND
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #                ↓
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #            100 nF to comparator (+)
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #            100 kΩ to same node
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #Total extra parts: 1× 100 nF, 3× resistors. The rest of LUMA2 stays unchanged.
10a2985f [claude-opus-4-5] #
