10a9adf8 [nitro@ryzen5] can i use a buffer chip instead of transceiver and 8 data gpio to write to low and high chips
10a9af85 [gemini-3-pro] #To drive a 16-bit bus using only 8 data GPIO pins, a standard buffer or transceiver (like the
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #74LVC245) is insufficient. You cannot present the full 16-bit word to the 68K simultaneously if the
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #pins are shared, as the low and high bytes would mirror each other or require precise timing that
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #the bus cycle may not support.
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #You require Octal Latches (such as the 74LVC573 or 74LVC374) instead of
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #standard transceivers.
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is how this configuration functions:
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Latching: You connect the 8 RP2040 data pins to the inputs of both chips. You write the
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #Low Byte and trigger the latch on the first chip, holding that data. You then write the High Byte
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #to the second chip.
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Presentation: Once both latches are loaded, the full 16-bit word is ready at the
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #outputs.
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #3.  Bus Isolation: You must control the Output Enable (/OE) pins to ensure the latches only
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #drive the Mega Drive bus when the address decode logic confirms a write cycle. This prevents bus
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #contention with the 68K.
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #• Summary: Use 2x 74LVC573* latches. This allows 8 data pins + 2 latch control pins
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #to drive the full 16-bit bus.
10a9af86 [gemini-3-pro] #
