10a9b3f1 [nitro@ryzen5] 74LS374 Ocatl Tri D Flip Flop IC are available at local jaycar NZ$3.00
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #Yes, you can use the 74LS374 to drive the 16-bit bus using only 8 GPIO data lines from the RP2040.
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #Since the 74LS374 is an Octal D-Type Flip-Flop (Latch), it has memory, whereas the 74LVC245
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #Transceiver does not. This latching capability is required if you intend to reuse the same 8 GPIO
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #pins to populate a 16-bit bus.
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #• Implementation Strategy:*
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Multiplexing: You can write the low byte (D0-D7) to the GPIOs and toggle the clock pin
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #on the first 74LS374 to latch the data. Then, write the high byte (D8-D15) to the same GPIOs and
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #toggle the clock on the second 74LS374.
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Bus Driving: Once both chips hold the correct data, you can assert the Output Enable
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #(OE) pins on both chips simultaneously to drive the full D0-D15 width onto the Megadrive bus during
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #the write cycle.
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #• Hardware Considerations:*
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #
10a9b567 [gemini-3-pro] #• Voltage Levels: The 74LS series runs on 5V. The RP2040 outputs 3.3V logic, which is
10a9b568 [gemini-3-pro] #typically high enough to register as a logic '1' on 74LS inputs (V_IH is usually 2.0V). However,
10a9b568 [gemini-3-pro] #ensure you do not read from the 74LS374 back into the RP2040, as the 5V output would damage the
10a9b568 [gemini-3-pro] #3.3V GPIOs.
10a9b568 [gemini-3-pro] #• Timing: Ensure your PIO timing accounts for the setup and hold times required to latch
10a9b568 [gemini-3-pro] #the data into the flip-flops before the 68K bus cycle expects valid data.
10a9b568 [gemini-3-pro] #
