10bab45e [nitro@ryzen5] big tidy
10bab70f [gemini-3-pro] #Hi, I have reviewed the Project Joy section of your WIP document (specifically the "big
10bab70f [gemini-3-pro] #tidy" revision at the bottom).
10bab70f [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab70f [gemini-3-pro] #Here are the specific technical points for review:
10bab70f [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab70f [gemini-3-pro] #1. Shift Register Daisy Chaining (Wiring)
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #In the "Shift In" section, you are chaining the registers using the inverted output:
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #	U1.7, /Q7, U2 DS`
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Issue: Connecting `/Q7` (Pin 7) to `DS` (Pin 10) will invert the data bits for every
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #subsequent register in the chain. U2 will be inverted, U3 normal, U4 inverted, etc.
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Fix: Connect Q7 (Pin 9) to DS (Pin 10) for standard serial chaining.
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #2. ADC Voltage Protection
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #You are using a CD4051 to multiplex joystick potentiometers (Apple II / PC) to the RP2040 ADC
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #(`GP26`, `GP27`).
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Issue: Vintage joystick potentiometers (especially Apple II) operate on 5V. The
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #RP2040 ADC is 3.3V tolerant. Feeding 5V directly from the MUX to the ADC will damage the
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #RP2040.
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Fix: Ensure the CD4051 is powered by 3.3V (clipping the signal range) or, if the MUX must
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #run at 5V, place a voltage divider (e.g., 2.2kΩ / 3.3kΩ) between the MUX Output and the RP2040 ADC
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #pin.
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #3. Apple II Inputs
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #You have Apple II inputs mapped to U5 (Shift Register):
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #	U5.11, P0, Apple2 Button0` ... `U5.12, P1, Apple2 Button1`
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Observation: Standard Apple II paddles/joysticks are analog (150kΩ potentiometers). They
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #cannot be read by a digital shift register (74HC165).
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Assumption: I assume U5 is intended for the Buttons (digital) and the MUX is for
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #the Paddles (analog).
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Note: If you intend to read the directionals (Up/Down) via U5, this only works for
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #non-standard "digital" Apple II controllers, not the standard analog sticks.
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #4. Latch Bit-Splitting (74LS374)
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #Your output latch mapping splits joysticks across chips to save pins:
10bab710 [gemini-3-pro] #• Joy 2 is split between U9 (Q5-Q7) and U10 (Q0-Q1).
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #• Joy 4 is split between U10 (Q7) and U11 (Q0-Q3).
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #• Review: This is electrically fine, but your software driver will need to perform
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #bit-masking and shifting to reconstruct the bytes before writing to the latches.
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #5. Shift Register Input Grounding
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #	U1.10, DS, GND`
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #• Review: Correct. The first device in the chain must have its Serial Input (DS) grounded
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #(or tied low) so that zeroes are shifted in behind the data.
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #6. Apple II Button Mapping
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #	U5.11, P0, Apple2 Button0`
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #• Pinout Check: Apple II DE9 Pin 1 is usually Button 0, Pin 2 is +5V. If you are using a
10bab711 [gemini-3-pro] #standard Apple II DE9 pinout, ensure your header wiring matches the shift register inputs.
