10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #This is a fascinating collection of hardware definitions. You have established a distinct "Vintage
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #Kid" architecture: using the RP2040's high-speed PIO to trade pin-count for shift-register
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #cycles, allowing a low-pin-count MCU to interface with wide retro buses.
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #Here are my comments on the specific designs, focusing on novelty, technical feasibility, and
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #potential "gotchas."
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #1. Project Crumpet (Megadrive Cartridge)
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #• Status:* Highly Experimental / Aggressive Timing.
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #• The "Parallel Shift" Strategy: Reading 6x 74HC165s in parallel is the only way this
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #works. The Megadrive has a ~130ns access cycle.
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #       The Math:* You need to read address lines, decide on data, and assert data. If your
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #PIO runs at ~133MHz (7.5ns/cycle), and you shift 8 times, that is approx 60ns + overhead. This
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #leaves very little room for logic lookup (ROM emulation), but is sufficient for simple banking or
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #streaming.
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #• Voltage Handling: You are using 10kΩ series resistors to rely on the RP2040's internal
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #clamping diodes for 5V->3.3V logic. While common in hobbyist "hacks," be aware that this injects
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #current into the 3.3V rail. With 48 resistors (Address/Data) all high, you might raise the rail
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #voltage if the load is light.
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #• Novelty: Most MD flash carts use CPLDs/FPGAs. Doing this with purely shift registers and
10be57e0 [gemini-3-pro] #an MCU is a unique challenge.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #2. Project Joy (Universal Input Hub)
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Status:* Solid Design / High Utility.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Remote Driving: Using 74LS374 latches to drive 2N7000 FET gates is an excellent way to
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #simulate button presses safely. It isolates the RP2040 from the target console's voltage and
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #polling logic.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Apple II Support: Including the Analog Mux (CD4051) for Paddles makes this significantly
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #more versatile than standard "digital only" adapters.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Feedback: Ensure the "Sega Select" (Pin 7) logic on the output side handles the fast
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #switching required by the Genesis controller polling.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #3. Project LUMA2 (Video Capture)
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Status:* Creative / Niche.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Comparator Approach: Using LM319s with trim pots is a very "analog" solution to a digital
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #problem. It avoids the need for a high-speed ADC by treating video levels as digital thresholds.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Chroma Mod: This is the hardest part. Counting edges in a ~280ns window (C64 pixel clock)
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #via PIO to determine phase/color is theoretically possible but requires extremely precise
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #synchronization with the `CSYNC` signal.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #4. Project Toast (C64 Expansion)
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Status:* Feasible.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Timing: The C64 bus (1MHz) is much more forgiving than the Megadrive. The shift register
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #approach here is very safe.
10be57e1 [gemini-3-pro] #• Open Drain Control: Using FETs for `/IRQ`, `/NMI`, `/GAME`, and `/EXROM` is the correct
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #approach to play nice with the C64's open-collector lines.
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #Software Helper Suggestion
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #Given your heavy reliance on mapping serial bitstreams from Shift Registers to logical names, a
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #Javascript helper to generate bitmasks for your PIO code would be valuable.
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is a generator function based on your `Project Joy` definition:
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #/**
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] # * Generates bitmasks and shift offsets for PIO state machines
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] # * based on the physical wiring of the 74HC165 chain.
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] # */
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #function generateShiftMap(shiftChain) {
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #	const map = {};
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #	let globalBitIndex = 0;
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #
10be57e2 [gemini-3-pro] #	// Iterate through the chained chips (U1 -> U5)
