10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #Here is a technical sanity check on your selected parts against the RP2040 logic levels and your
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #specific project requirements:
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #1. 74LS374 (Octal Flip-Flop)
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #• Application: Output latches (Crumpet, Joy).
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #• Compatibility: Good.
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #    *   You will power these with 5V (VBUS).
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #    *   The RP2040 (3.3V) can successfully drive the inputs of the "LS" series (Low-power
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #Schottky). LS logic considers anything over 2.0V as a HIGH, so the 3.3V output from the Pico is
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #well within range.
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Note: LS chips consume more power than CMOS (HC/HCT) chips, but for a mains-powered
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #console or USB-powered hub, this is negligible.
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #2. 74HC165 (Shift Register)
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #• Application: Parallel Inputs (Crumpet, Joy, Node, Toast).
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #• Compatibility: Good (with your design).
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #    *   You will power these with 3.3V (3V3).
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Crucial: Your design includes 10kΩ series resistors on the inputs. This is
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #essential because you are feeding 5V signals (from the consoles) into a chip running at 3.3V. The
10be955a [gemini-3-pro] #resistors limit the current into the internal clamping diodes of the 74HC165, protecting the chip
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #and the RP2040. Without the resistors, the magic smoke would escape.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #3. 74LVC245 (Transceiver)
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #• Application: Bidirectional Data Bus / Buffers (Crumpet, Node, Toast).
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #• Compatibility: Perfect.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #    *   The "LVC" family is the gold standard for 3.3V <-> 5V interfacing.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #    *   They are 5V tolerant on inputs even when powered at 3.3V.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #    *   They are fast enough for the Megadrive and C64 bus speeds.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #4. 2N7000 (N-Channel FET)
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #• Application: Open Drain logic (driving lines LOW).
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #• Compatibility: Good.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #    *   The Gate Threshold Voltage (V_GS(th)) of a 2N7000 is typically between 0.8V and 3.0V.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #    *   The RP2040 drives 3.3V, which is enough to fully switch these FETs "ON" to pull the signal
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #lines to Ground.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #    *   This is a very robust way to handle "Active Low" signals on vintage buses without risking
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #voltage contention.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #5. 10K Piher Trimpot
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #• Application: Luma2 Video Thresholds.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #• Compatibility: Good.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Piher is a reputable brand; these horizontal mounts are physically stable, which is helpful
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #for video tuning where vibration might otherwise cause flickering.
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #Summary
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #Your "Jaycar Draft" is technically sound. You have navigated the voltage mismatch between the
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #modern RP2040 (3.3V) and vintage hardware (5V) correctly by mixing logic families (LS for 5V
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #output, LVC for bus buffering, HC at 3.3V for inputs).
10be955b [gemini-3-pro] #
