10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #Yes, Luma2 has plenty of spare GPIO. The RP2040 exposes 26 GPIO pins (plus inputs on the
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #ADC pins).
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #Your current Luma2 allocation uses roughly 10 pins, leaving 16 pins free.
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #Regarding your technical questions:
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #1. Stereo Audio
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Yes.* You can simply enable `GP27` as `ADC1` for the second channel.
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #• Note: The RP2040 ADC inputs are 0V to 3.3V. Audio signals are AC (swinging positive and
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #negative). You will need a simple DC Bias circuit (two resistors and a capacitor) to lift the audio
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #signal to ~1.65V so the Pico can sample the full wave without clipping the negative voltages.
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #2. Chroma: Edge Counting vs More Bits
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #For the Commodore 64 specifically, Edge Counting (1-bit Chroma) is "good enough" to get 90%
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #of the way there, but it has limits.
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #• The Physics:*
10bf4279 [gemini-3-pro] #C64 Color (PAL/NTSC) is defined by two things:
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Phase (Hue): Where the wave starts relative to the "Burst".
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Amplitude (Saturation): How "tall" the wave is.
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• Edge Counting (1 Comparator):*
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #This turns the sine wave into a square wave.
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• Pros: You capture the Phase perfectly. The PIO counts clocks between edges to
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #determine if it's Red, Purple, Green, etc.
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• Cons: You lose Amplitude. You cannot tell the difference between a "strong" red
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #and a "weak" red, or—more importantly—between "Grey" (No color) and "Color".
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• Workaround: The PIO can detect "No Signal" (flat line) vs "Signal" (toggling). Since the
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #C64 palette is limited (16 colors), and the Greys/White/Black are distinct Luma levels, you can
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #usually infer the correct color just by combining Luma Level + Phase.
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• The "More Bits" Solution (2 Comparators):*
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #If you want to be precise, you add a second comparator to the Chroma line with a higher voltage
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #threshold.
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• Comparator A (Zero Cross): Detects Phase.
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• Comparator B (Threshold): Detects Saturation (only triggers on strong color signals).
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #• Verdict: For a C64, this is likely overkill. The 5-level Luma slicer you have designed is
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #the most important part. If you get Luma right, 1-bit Chroma edge counting is sufficient.
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf427a [gemini-3-pro] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
