10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #Yes, the RP2040 has enough GPIO for Luma2 even with the Waveshare 1.14" LCD and
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #Stereo Audio added, but it requires a "Tetris-style" pin re-allocation because the LCD HAT
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #hard-wires specific pins.
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #Chroma Sampling Logic
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #Regarding your worry about Chroma bits:
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Phase (Hue): The C64 generates color by changing the phase of the subcarrier
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #relative to the color burst. A single high-speed comparator (1-bit) is actually optimal here. The
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #PIO can time the distance between zero-crossings (edges) of the burst and the signal. More bits
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #won't help much with phase detection; edge precision is king.
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Amplitude (Saturation): While C64 colors do have different saturations, the Phase is
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #the primary identifier. Adding bits here would require a flash ADC (resistor ladder), consuming 3-4
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #more pins.
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #3.  Verdict: Stick to Edge Counting with one comparator. It is "as good as it gets"
10bf4b96 [gemini-3-pro] #for a digital GPIO approach without an external video decoder chip.
