10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #Yes, Luma2 has plenty of spare GPIO.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #The RP2040 exposes 26 GPIO pins. Your current Luma2 spec uses roughly 10 pins.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #You have enough room for Stereo Audio (ADC), the Chroma comparator, and an SPI screen.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is the breakdown of your questions regarding Chroma, the LCD, and the pinout strategy.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #1. Chroma Sampling: Edge Counting vs. More Comparators
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #For the Commodore 64 (and most 8-bit composite signals), Edge Counting (Phase Detection) is the
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #correct approach, not more voltage comparators.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #• How C64 Color Works: The color is determined by the Phase (timing delay) of the
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #AC sine wave relative to the "Color Burst" sync pulse.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #• Edge Counting: A single comparator turns the Chroma sine wave into a square wave. The
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #RP2040 PIO is fast enough (running at >125MHz) to measure the exact time between the Sync pulse and
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #the Chroma rising edge.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #• More Comparators? Adding more comparators would measure Saturation (how vivid the
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #color is vs grey). While technically "better," a single comparator is standard for retro upscalers
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #because the C64 has a fixed palette; if you detect the phase, you know which of the 16 colors it
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #is.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #• Verdict:* Stick to the single comparator for Chroma. It is simpler and relies on the
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #PIO's timing precision, which is the RP2040's strength.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #2. What is IPS?
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #• IPS (In-Plane Switching)* is a type of LCD panel technology.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #• Old screens (TN): If you look at them from an angle, the colors invert or turn black.
10c06f97 [gemini-3-pro] #• IPS screens: You can look at the screen from almost any angle (up, down, side), and the
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #colors stay accurate and vibrant.
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #• Relevance: The Waveshare 1.14" is an IPS display. It looks excellent and is perfect for a
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #handheld or small monitor project.
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #3. Waveshare 1.14" LCD Integration
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #The Waveshare 1.14" Pico Module is a "Hat" that plugs directly onto the Pico headers. It uses a
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #specific set of pins for the SPI interface and 4 buttons.
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #• Resolution Warning: The 1.14" LCD is 240x135* pixels.
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #The C64 is 320x200.
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #• You will need to downscale (skip pixels) to fit the image on this screen, or show a cropped
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #window.
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #Revised Luma2 + LCD Pinout
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #To make Luma2 work with the Waveshare LCD, we must avoid the pins the LCD uses (GP8-GP13 for
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #screen, GP15/GP20 for buttons).
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #• Conflict:* Your original Luma2 spec used GP16-GP22. This is actually perfect and mostly
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #avoids the LCD, except for the LCD buttons which we can ignore or repurpose.
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
10c06f98 [gemini-3-pro] #
