10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #The Waffle project appears to be a 3.3V GBA cartridge adapter using similar shift register and
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #transceiver logic as the Toast C64 project, but with key differences:
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #• Key similarities:*
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #- Both use 74HC165 shift registers for input capture
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #- Both use 74LVC245 transceivers for data bus output
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #- Both interface RP2040 with vintage gaming hardware
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #• Key differences:*
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #1. Voltage levels: Waffle is 3.3V native (GBA), while Toast needs 5V interfacing
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #2. Bus architecture: GBA uses multiplexed address/data bus (AD0-AD15), C64 has separate
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #address and data buses
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #3. Control signals: Different sets (GBA: /CS, /RD, /WR; C64: /ROML, /ROMH, /IRQ, etc.)
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #4. Clock speed: GBA PHI ~16.78MHz vs C64 PHI2 ~0.985MHz
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #5. No open-drain drivers: Waffle doesn't need FETs since it's 3.3V to 3.3V
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #• Notable Waffle features:*
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #- Save memory interface (GP11-14) for SRAM/EEPROM/Flash
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #- Bank latch control (GP10) for larger ROMs
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #- Direct control signal connections (GP7-9) without shift registers
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #The Waffle design seems cleaner due to matching voltage levels, though the multiplexed bus adds
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #complexity. Both projects demonstrate clever use of shift registers to expand GPIO capabilities.
10ca33cf [deepseek-chat] #
