10cb6057 [gemini-3-pro] #Here is the design review for the provided netlists.
10cb6057 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6057 [gemini-3-pro] #Project Toast (C64)
10cb6057 [gemini-3-pro] #• Status:* Functional Logic, Critical Timing Failure.
10cb6057 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6057 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  The Latency Killer (Critical):
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   You are reading the Address Bus (A0-A15) via Shift Registers (74HC165).
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   C64 PHI2 clock is ~1MHz (~1000ns cycle). The valid data window after address stabilization
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #is much shorter.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   To read an address, you must latch, then clock 32 bits (4 registers) via SPI.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Even at a wildly optimistic 50MHz SPI clock, the transfer overhead + ISR latency + logic
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #lookup will exceed the C64's access window. The C64 will read garbage before the RP2040 can assert
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #the correct data.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #       Fix:* Address lines must be read via direct GPIO (Parallel) or PIO to meet 1MHz bus
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #timing.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Transceiver Direction Hardwired:
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Netlist 5.1: `DIR` is tied to `3V3`.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   This locks the 74LVC245 to Output Only (RP2040 -> C64).
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   You cannot read the C64 data bus through this transceiver. While you have Shift Register 3
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #for reading D0-D7, you cannot use the transceiver to isolate the bus during read cycles if the
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #direction is fixed to drive.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #3.  Voltage Protection:
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Using 10k series resistors with 74HC165 at 3.3V relies on the chip's internal clamping
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #diodes to sink the 5V C64 logic.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #       Slop:* It works for hackers, but it's technically out of spec.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #       Fix:* Use 74LVC165A, which has 5V tolerant inputs even when powered at 3.3V.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #Project Waffle (GBA)
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #• Status:* Architecture Mismatch.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #1.  Timing Impossibility:
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   GBA bus runs at 16.78 MHz (~60ns cycle).
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Using shift registers to interface with this bus is impossible for emulation. The bus is
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #16x faster than the C64, and the shift register approach was already too slow for the C64.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #2.  Bus Width Mismatch:
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   GBA is a 16-bit system (AD0-AD15).
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   Your GPIO allocation only accounts for 8 bits of data (`GP15-22: DATA_BUS D0-D7`). You
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #effectively sever half the bus, making most ROMs unreadable/unplayable.
10cb6058 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #3.  Unnecessary Hardware:
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   The RP2040 has 26 usable GPIOs.
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #    *   GBA Interface requires: 16 (AD0-15) + 4 Control (/CS, /RD, /WR, PHI) = 20 Pins.
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #       Fix:* Remove all shift registers. Wire the GBA cartridge port directly to the RP2040
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #GPIOs (with level shifters if strictly necessary, though many run RP2040 inputs with simple
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #resistors on 3.3V GBA logic).
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #Summary
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #• Project Toast* needs direct parallel inputs for Address lines to work as an emulator.
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #• Project Waffle* should be scrapped and routed as Direct GPIO; the shift register
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #architecture is too slow by orders of magnitude.
10cb6059 [gemini-3-pro] #
